                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_1, p_2
                  as in the following diagram (
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo ,
                        %mi i
                        %mo ′
                        %mo ,
                        %mi i
                        %mo ″
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i, i', i''
                  are inclusion maps, all taking a domain element
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                  to itself):
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mrow
                          %mtable
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                                %msup
                                  %mi g
                                  %mrow
                                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                                    %mn 1
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
